import{_ as t,o,c as a,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.jdsM-LKF.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Design decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","filePath":"explanation/design-decisions.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"explanation/design-decisions.md"};function r(i,e,d,l,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="Design-decisions" tabindex="-1">Design decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Design-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design decisions {#Design-decisions}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The decisions that shape FFTL, each with the reason and the evidence it rests on. A decision stays here until something replaces it; a replaced decision is marked, so a reader who finds the old reasoning elsewhere can see that it no longer holds. Numbers are measurements on an Apple M1 Max and on AMD EPYC (Zen 2) unless a line says otherwise.</p><h2 id="Licence-and-provenance" tabindex="-1">Licence and provenance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Licence-and-provenance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Licence and provenance {#Licence-and-provenance}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> FFTL is MIT-licensed. No GPL code is read, copied, translated or closely paraphrased. FFTW is a benchmark competitor only. Concepts known from the literature are used; code structure, naming and style deliberately do not follow FFTW (a 25-year-old C library with generated codelets and a measuring planner). Permissive sources may be read; a close take-over keeps its notice. The rules for contributors are on the <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR29/howto/contribute#Contribute">Contribute</a> page and in <code>AGENTS.md</code>.</p><h2 id="Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" tabindex="-1">Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Codelets with complex values in SIMD registers {#Codelets-with-complex-values-in-SIMD-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> A codelet works on complex values interleaved in SIMD registers. On aarch64 with the ARMv8.3 complex-number extension it uses <code>FCADD</code> and <code>FCMLA</code>, elsewhere shuffles (x86: <code>vfmaddsub</code> for products, <code>addsub</code> for ±i). Whether the extension is present is decided when the package is compiled: Apple silicon always, Linux by the <code>fcma</code> flag in <code>/proc/cpuinfo</code> (LLVM&#39;s host feature query returns nothing on macOS).</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> On the M1 this layout with <code>FCADD</code>/<code>FCMLA</code> was the first variant that beat FFTW; keeping split real and imaginary parts in registers spilled for <code>Float64</code>. The layout is a per-platform choice behind dispatch on the vector type, so the generator and the plan structure stay shared.</p><h2 id="A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" tabindex="-1">A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner <a class="header-anchor" href="#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A heuristic radix choice, no measuring planner {#A-heuristic-radix-choice,-no-measuring-planner}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision.</strong> The radix sequence of a length comes from a fixed rule on its prime factorisation (<code>src/factor.jl</code>). There is no measuring planner by default.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Brute-force searches over all decompositions put the best and the second-best within 1–3 %, and the large losses of an estimate-only planner are cache effects the algorithm has to avoid anyway. A per-machine calibration or an optional measuring mode can follow if the rule leaves too much on the table.</p><p>One such cache effect is part of the rule: single lines on AVX-512 take radix 16 only while the 16 streams of a stage are less than an L1 set stride (4 KB) apart, that is up to 4096 for <code>Float32</code> and 2048 for <code>Float64</code>. Beyond that the streams share one cache set and radix 8 wins. AMD EPYC 9354, one line, all buffers at fixed page offsets, time of the radix-16 sequence over the radix-8 sequence: <code>Float32</code> 4096: 0.88, 8192: 1.06, 16384: 1.06; <code>Float64</code> 4096: 1.33, 8192: 1.03, 16384: 1.11.</p><h2 id="Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" tabindex="-1">Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others <a class="header-anchor" href="#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Rules for lengths are derived on some lengths and checked on others {#Rules-for-lengths-are-derived-on-some-lengths-and-checked-on-others}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-04).</strong> The radix choice and the grid length of a resampled transform are rules on the prime factorisation, not tables per length. A rule is derived from one part of the lengths (a brute-force search over all decompositions, or a cost model) and checked on lengths that were not used for the derivation, for example by parity of n or by a random split; only the checked result counts as evidence. A mechanism with a stated cause (a cost estimate) is preferred over a list of lengths. Rules are per architecture where the search differs. After every change of a rule, the dense sweep is repeated and its distribution (median, share above 1.2×, 1.5×, 2×, worst lengths) is the criterion, not single points.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The first rules were fitted to a few dozen lengths, mostly powers of two and smooth lengths on the M1. A dense sweep of every length 2…1024 against FFTW MEASURE (one thread, Zen 2, 2046 cases) showed 100 <code>Float32</code> and 64 <code>Float64</code> lengths slower than 1.5×, the worst at 3.4×.</p><h2 id="Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-resample-the-rest" tabindex="-1">Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, resample the rest <a class="header-anchor" href="#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-resample-the-rest" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lengths with large prime factors: split off the smooth part, resample the rest {#Lengths-with-large-prime-factors:-split-off-the-smooth-part,-resample-the-rest}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-07).</strong> For n = s·q, with q the part made of primes above <code>MAX_RADIX_PRIME</code> (73, at the time of the decision 31): one Cooley–Tukey step, a chain of length s, twiddles, and s resampled transforms of length q (<code>Composite</code>): one batched pass from 32 lanes on, lane by lane below (2026-11-12). A leftover q stays one resampled transform while its grid is at most 4 MiB; above that, q is split into its prime powers, recursively. Grids of 8 MiB and more are transformed by a serial four-step algorithm on x86 (1.3× from 2²⁰ on, Zen 2) but not on aarch64, where the chain is faster up to 2²² (M1 Max). With threads, <code>CompositeMT</code> is used when s is at least the number of threads and is bitwise equal to the serial kernel; otherwise the whole length is one <code>ResampleMT</code>.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The grids of length about 2q stay in cache. On 45 non-smooth lengths (Zen 2) FFTL moved from ×0.64 to ×1.04 of the fastest other library with one thread, and from ×0.77 to ×1.16 with 16 threads (measured with Bluestein&#39;s algorithm on the leftover). Resampling needs one transform of a length of at least 2q where Bluestein&#39;s algorithm needs two: it took 0.51–0.82 of Bluestein&#39;s time for primes from 1009 to 100003 (Tiger Lake, one thread), at an error of 2–5·10⁻¹⁵ in <code>Float64</code> (Bluestein about 5·10⁻¹⁶). The batched pass vectorises across the lanes and loses with few of them: against the lanes one by one it took 1.7–3.8 times as long at s = 2, 1.1–1.6 at s = 7, 0.73–1.04 at s = 16 and 0.56–1.04 at s = 32 (<code>Float64</code>, q = 37, 1009, 10007, EPYC 9354, one thread; <code>Float32</code> 2.1–7.3, 1.6–2.9, 0.84–1.31, 0.87–0.96). The switch is on the lane count and not on the number of threads, which keeps serial and threaded plans bitwise equal.</p><p>The grid of a single resampled transform has a margin on each side that holds copies of the other end (2026-11-12), so every output is interpolated from one contiguous load. Without it the outputs whose window wraps around the grid ends (7 per <code>Float64</code> transform, 3 per <code>Float32</code>) took a scalar loop at about ten times the cost of the others: 250 of 559 ns at n = 37 and 290 of 1104 ns at n = 101. With the margin, time over the time without it: n = 37: 0.55, 101: 0.75, 127: 0.79, 1009: 0.96, 100003: 0.98, 101³: 0.78; against <code>main</code>: 0.81, 0.81, 0.77, 0.64, 0.80, 0.83, and 0.85 for 101³ on 8 threads (1.17 before). <code>Float64</code>, EPYC 9354, minima of interleaved runs, the same code against itself within 6 %. The margin is a whole page: a margin of 128 bytes moves the grid off the page offset of the work buffers, and the prime length 100003 then took 1.03–1.05 of the time without margins.</p><p>Against Bluestein&#39;s algorithm with the looped prime kernel in both (2026-11-14; <code>main</code> at 540d876, AMD EPYC 9354, one NUMA node of a shared machine, one process per variant and thread count, 3 rounds in rotating order, a second copy of <code>main</code> as A/A): over 73 one-thread cases (1-D lines up to 10⁷, planes, 3-D, <code>rfft</code>, run-time plans, composite dimensions) the geometric mean of the time ratio is 0.74, 55 cases faster beyond the A/A band and none slower; over 30–31 cases at 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32 threads 0.83, 0.66, 0.68, 0.68 and 0.66, none slower beyond the band (A/A median deviation 5–23 %, largest at 2, 4 and 32 threads). The seven cases with a median above 1.05 were measured again in 7 rounds: 0.60–1.01. 1009·2¹⁶ (<code>Float64</code>, 16 threads) took 165 ms instead of 552–555 ms: on <code>main</code> the second step, lane by lane, took 445 of 554 ms, almost all of it in writing each output 2¹⁶ values from the last (107 ms with the same outputs written contiguously); the batched pass writes runs of consecutive outputs. Error against FFTW in <code>Float64</code> precision, largest over the cases: <code>Float64</code> 3.3·10⁻¹⁵ (Bluestein 1.0·10⁻¹⁵), <code>Float32</code> 1.8·10⁻⁷ (Bluestein 3.1·10⁻⁷).</p><p><strong>Replaced.</strong> Bluestein&#39;s algorithm on the leftover and <code>BluesteinMT</code> for a whole line on threads (2026-11-07). Generic number types and the GPU passes keep Bluestein&#39;s algorithm.</p><p><strong>Not done / not checked.</strong> Rader&#39;s algorithm (5 of the 45 sweep lengths qualify). Both thresholds were measured on Zen 2 only, and the 4 MiB rule is checked on 12 lengths that also took part in its derivation. The 32-lane rule was measured on one AVX-512 machine only, not on AVX2 or aarch64, and the grid margin on x86 only (512-bit and 256-bit vectors), not on aarch64.</p><h2 id="Plan-tables-of-long-lines-from-a-few-exact-values" tabindex="-1">Plan tables of long lines from a few exact values <a class="header-anchor" href="#Plan-tables-of-long-lines-from-a-few-exact-values" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plan tables of long lines from a few exact values {#Plan-tables-of-long-lines-from-a-few-exact-values}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-14).</strong> Two tables that a plan builds over the indices of a long line no longer take one transcendental call per entry:</p><ul><li><p>The deconvolution factors of a resampled transform (<code>resample_corr</code>) are a sum of 26 (<code>Float64</code>) or 18 (<code>Float32</code>) cosines over the quadrature nodes, each sampled at consecutive indices t. With t = t0 + k (t0 a multiple of 64), every cosine is one product of two correctly rounded values: one <code>sincospi</code> per node and block of 64, and a table over k shared by all blocks. The node weights a_q = w_q ψ(z_q) are computed once, not per index, and the factors are even in t, so half of them are computed.</p></li><li><p>The factor tables of a long four-step (above <code>TUNING[].factor_twiddles</code>) take their n1 + n2 roots from <code>unit_root</code>, which applies the symmetries of <code>unit_roots</code> to one index. The tables are bitwise the same as before, without the table of all n roots they were taken from (2 GiB for 2²⁷).</p></li></ul><p><strong>Why.</strong> Planning a prime length near 2²⁶ took 32 s, 30 of them in the deconvolution factors (67·10⁶ indices, 26 <code>exp</code>, <code>sqrt</code> and <code>cospi</code> each). AMD EPYC 9354, 16 threads, a fresh plan with the cache cleared, 2 rounds in alternating processes, before → after:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">length</th><th style="text-align:left;">kernel</th><th style="text-align:left;">plan</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">2²⁶ − 5</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>ResampleMT</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">32.0–32.8 s → 0.70 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">2²⁷</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>FourStep</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">1.65–1.69 s → 0.003 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">2²⁶</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>FourStep</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">0.81 s → 0.002 s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">100003</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>ResampleMT</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">0.041 s → 0.006 s</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>resample_corr</code> alone for 2²⁶ − 5 on one thread: 30.2 s → 0.68 s (<code>Float32</code> 0.43 s). Against 256-bit arithmetic (the same quadrature rule; n from 2 to 2²⁶ − 5, both ends of the index range and random indices), the product form has the error of one <code>cospi</code> per term: median 2.4–2.7 ulp, at most 8.9 ulp in <code>Float64</code> for both forms; in <code>Float32</code> the factors are bitwise the same for n up to 10⁶. A rotation recurrence (one complex multiply per step) is as fast but its error grows along the block: 1.2·10⁻¹³ after 512 steps. Transform times are unchanged (the four-step&#39;s output is bitwise the same).</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> The twiddles of the composite kernel (<code>Composite.tw</code>, <code>CompositeMT.tw</code>) are still a table of all n roots: 1 GiB and 1.4 s of planning for 1009·2¹⁶. The kernel reads it at run time, so factor tables there change the kernel.</p><h2 id="One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data" tabindex="-1">One chain form: the radix sequence is data <a class="header-anchor" href="#One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;One chain form: the radix sequence is data {#One-chain-form:-the-radix-sequence-is-data}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-13).</strong> Every smooth length runs on <code>RChain</code>: the radix sequence and the stage records are data, the codelets come from a fixed set. There is no chain type with the radix sequence in its type, and nothing is compiled per length. <code>RChain</code> is also the only form the trimmed C library can contain.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The compiled chain (<code>Chain{T,Rs,σ,K}</code>, used for smooth lengths up to 128 until this decision) existed because the run-time chain cost 9–20 ns more per call on x86. That cost was not the choice of the codelet at run time (about 1 ns per stage) but the code around it: GC frames for references loaded from the chain, the copy of an immutable chain into each caller, bounds checks, and one more call level per stage. With those removed (see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR29/explanation/implementation#Implementation">Implementation</a>) the compiled chain has no advantage left. AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), one thread, <code>plan_fft</code> and <code>mul!</code>, 92 cases (<code>Float64</code> and <code>Float32</code>; lines of 2 to 4096, planes of 8 × 8 to 128 × 128, cubes of 8³ to 64³, in-place, backward and real transforms), 8 rounds in alternating processes, median time over the median time with the compiled chain:</p><ul><li><p>geometric mean 0.973; 27 cases below 0.95, 8 above 1.05;</p></li><li><p>lines of one stage (2, 4, 16): 0.80–0.95; 8 × 8, 8 × 8 × 8, 7 × 12, 5 × 64: 0.85–0.91;</p></li><li><p>the cases above 1.05 are short lines of two or three stages: <code>Float32</code> 20, 32, 96 and 100 (1.09–1.10), 37 rows of 64 (1.13), <code>rfft</code> of 64 (1.07 and 1.09), and <code>Float64</code> 12 × 7 (1.05). The stage loop runs about 50 instructions per stage around the codelet (no mispredicted branches), 2.4 ns per stage against direct calls of the two kernels of <code>Float32</code> 32.</p></li><li><p>The machine was shared during the measurement: over the 8 rounds the slowest time of the compiled chain over its fastest is 1.04 in the median over the cases, 1.19 at the 90th percentile and 1.55 at most.</p></li></ul><p>Not verified on aarch64.</p><p>What the single form removes: 583 compiled chain methods and their generators, the dispatch trees from an <code>RChain</code> of a short length into the compiled chains, and the compilation at the first plan of a short length with a radix sequence outside the compiled set (a short dimension with a large stride). The package has 1796 methods instead of 2385, the package image on this machine is 61 MB instead of 72 MB, and <code>libfftl.so</code> (x86-64-v3, <code>--trim=safe</code>) is 49.5 MB instead of 55.6 MB.</p><p><strong>Replaced.</strong> The two chain forms (2026-11-09: compiled <code>Chain</code> for smooth lengths up to 128, <code>RChain</code> for the rest), and before that the idea of removing <code>Chain</code> without changing the stage loop (2026-11-08) and the separate JIT and run-time plan families (2026-11-02).</p><h2 id="Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" tabindex="-1">Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not <code>@generated</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kernels of a fixed set are plain methods, not `@generated` {#Kernels-of-a-fixed-set-are-plain-methods,-not-@generated}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10, fallback removed 2026-11-13).</strong> The stage kernels of the codelet set that <code>RChain</code> dispatches to are plain methods, defined with <code>@eval</code> in <code>src/kernel_methods.jl</code> from the functions that build their bodies. One table (<code>STAGE_KERNELS</code> in <code>src/kernels.jl</code>) lists the kernels and their arguments; the methods and the dispatch switches of <code>RChain</code> are made from it. There are no <code>@generated</code> stage kernels: every combination the chain can select has a plain method, so a fallback would never run.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A package image keeps the complete optimised IR of every precompiled <code>@generated</code> specialisation, 130 MB of the 146 MB image. See <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR29/explanation/image-size#Package-size-and-load-time">Package size and load time</a>.</p><h2 id="The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" tabindex="-1">The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The first stage of a single line runs at full vector width {#The-first-stage-of-a-single-line-runs-at-full-vector-width}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> Where the widest vector holds 8 complex values (<code>Float32</code> on AVX-512), the first stage of a single line runs <code>stage_small!</code>: vectorised over the butterflies, with the outputs interleaved in registers into contiguous stores. It exists for the power-of-two radices and belongs to the fixed set of plain methods.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> The grouped first stage handles at most 4 complex values per vector. Intel Core i7-1185G7, one line, <code>Float32</code>, lengths 128–16384 with radices of 16s and 8s: 12–26 % faster (the same code against itself differs by up to 3 %).</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> The same for later stages with a stride of 2 or 4. It needs a per-lane twiddle table and one kernel per stride, and only <code>Float32</code> lengths whose radices start with 4, 4 have such a stage. On an AMD EPYC 9354 it gained 6–8 % at 192 and 320 and nothing measurable at other lengths.</p><h2 id="Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" tabindex="-1">Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stage kernels keep twiddles in memory and address streams from few registers {#Stage-kernels-keep-twiddles-in-memory-and-address-streams-from-few-registers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-12).</strong> In the stages vectorised over q, the twiddle multiply reads re(w) and im(w) from the table (<code>ccmul_at</code>; memory operands on x86), and the R input and R output streams are addressed as base + odd·stride·2^e from one base per 8 streams (<code>stream_addr</code>). The base pointer is hidden from LLVM&#39;s loop optimiser (<code>opaque_ptr</code>), which otherwise goes back to one pointer per stream.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A radix-16 stage has 15 twiddles and 32 streams, more than fit in registers, so its inner loop reloaded vectors and pointers from the stack. AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), instructions per butterfly: radix 16 middle stage 284 → 198, radix 8 100 → 79. One line, <code>plan_fft</code>, medians of 8 interleaved rounds: lengths 512–16384 take 0.79–0.94 (<code>Float32</code>) and 0.82–0.92 (<code>Float64</code>) of the time, lengths from 32768 on are unchanged (the same code against itself differs by 1–5 %). Not verified on aarch64.</p><h2 id="The-prime-radices-from-17-to-73-share-one-looped-kernel" tabindex="-1">The prime radices from 17 to 73 share one looped kernel <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-prime-radices-from-17-to-73-share-one-looped-kernel" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The prime radices from 17 to 73 share one looped kernel {#The-prime-radices-from-17-to-73-share-one-looped-kernel}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-13).</strong> A stage of a prime radix from 17 on runs <code>prime_stage!</code> (<code>src/primestage.jl</code>): one kernel for all such radices, with the radix and a table of cosines and sines as arguments, compiled once per vector type. It uses the symmetric form of the odd-prime codelet as loops and computes up to eight output pairs (four with 16 vector registers) in one pass over the inputs. It covers the stage kinds that the codelets have separate kernels for: stages vectorised over the lanes, the last stage, lane blocks, and the grouped first stage. <code>MAX_RADIX_PRIME</code> is 73. The radices up to 16 keep their straight-line codelets. The KernelAbstractions kernels and the generic number types keep their limit of 31.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A straight-line codelet of a prime r has about r²/2 statements with as many different constants, which do not fit in registers (525 statements for 31), and every radix needed 82 kernel methods on AVX-512 (kind × sign × vector type). The codelets of 17–31 were therefore compiled at first use and not with the package (decision of 2026-11-10, replaced by this one). With the loop, a prime costs a table.</p><p>Measurements: AMD EPYC 9354 (AVX-512), one thread, <code>plan_fft</code> and <code>mul!</code>, rounds in alternating processes, median time over the median time of the previous code.</p><ul><li><p>Lengths and shapes with a factor 17–31 against the codelets (40 cases, 4 rounds): geometric mean 0.82, 36 cases below 0.95, one above 1.05 (<code>Float64</code> 100 × 31, 1.06: a single stage in place, which copies the inputs of a butterfly first). 17 and 31 alone: 0.63–0.84; 1088 = 17·64: 0.96 and 0.89; 837 = 27·31 and 841 = 29²: 0.72–0.82; 62 × 62: 0.70–0.74.</p></li><li><p>Long lengths (36 cases, 3 rounds; 31·2⁶ to 17·2²⁰, and a factor 17–31 times a prime above 1000): geometric mean 0.94, one case above 1.05 (<code>Float64</code> 19·2¹⁷, 1.08). 31·2⁶ to 31·2¹⁴: 0.74–0.91; 17·2²⁰: 0.99 and 1.01.</p></li><li><p>The primes 37–73 against Bluestein&#39;s algorithm, which they used before (100 cases, 2 rounds; p, 8p, 64p, 1024p, 16384p): geometric mean 0.29. The prime alone: 0.43–0.79 (<code>Float64</code>) and 0.48–0.90 (<code>Float32</code>); as a factor of a longer length: 0.13–0.53.</p></li><li><p>92 general cases without such factors (the set of the decision above, 4 rounds): geometric mean 1.00 without the two cases with a dimension of 37 (0.10 and 0.16), 3 cases above 1.05 (<code>Float64</code> lines of 32, 48 and 60: 1.07–1.15). In 6 rounds on 20 short lines alone these three were at 1.04, 0.83 and 1.05 and the geometric mean 1.00; the stage kernels of these lengths compile to the same machine code as before.</p></li><li><p>Accuracy against a 200-bit reference, <code>Float64</code>, 841, 6256 and 6859: 1.4–1.8 ε, with the codelets 3.5–4.6 ε (the table is built from angles reduced to one turn).</p></li></ul><p>What made the loop as fast as the unrolled sums: several rows per pass, so that the inputs are loaded once for all of them and the multiply-add chains of the rows run side by side; no buffer for the sums and differences of the inputs (storing whole registers was the slowest part: 1.3–1.4× per butterfly with the buffer); the pass over a block of rows out of line and the outputs of a block written by recursion over the tuples of accumulators (a loop with an index into them, or a closure over them, put the accumulators on the stack: up to 1.4× for a whole transform).</p><p><strong>The limit.</strong> With the limit at 127 for the measurement, a prime from 79 on is still 1.3–4× faster than Bluestein&#39;s algorithm as a factor of a longer length, but the line of that prime alone is slower from 79 on for <code>Float32</code> (1.05, 1.5 at 127) and from 97 on for <code>Float64</code> (1.01, 1.15 at 127). 73 is the largest prime for which every measured case is faster.</p><p>Against resampling, which replaced Bluestein&#39;s algorithm (2026-11-14, EPYC 9354, one thread, the kernels called directly, median of 3 rounds): as a factor of 64p the looped kernel takes 0.29–0.44 of the composite kernel&#39;s time for every prime p from 37 to 73, in both precisions. A line of the prime alone in <code>Float64</code> takes 0.59–0.97 of the resampled time (0.97 at 73). In <code>Float32</code> resampling is faster from 47 on: the looped kernel takes 1.10–1.96 times as long (1.96 at 73), at 37–43 they are even (0.91–1.08). The looped kernel takes the same time in both precisions, the resampled transform 0.49–0.64 of its <code>Float64</code> time in <code>Float32</code>. The limit stays at 73; a rule for <code>Float32</code> lines of a prime alone is not done.</p><p><strong>Not done.</strong> The loop for 11 and 13: with one kernel per radix (an earlier form of the loop) it was at 0.98 of the codelets for <code>Float64</code> and 1.05–1.06 for <code>Float32</code>, and 1.08–1.10 for 7; not measured with the single kernel. A larger limit for lengths that are not a prime alone. aarch64 is not verified.</p><p><strong>Costs.</strong> The kernel and its tables are in the package image, which the lazy codelets were not: the image file is 66 MB instead of 59 MB on this machine (68.5 MB before the decision on the chain form). The C library contains every kernel in either case and shrinks: <code>libfftl.so</code> (x86-64-v3, <code>--trim=safe</code>) is 26.8 MB instead of 49.5 MB, its code 8.8 MB instead of 18.6 MB.</p><h2 id="Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" tabindex="-1">Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Run-time pass plans for trimmed programs {#Run-time-pass-plans-for-trimmed-programs}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-10).</strong> A pass plan (<code>plan_pass</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass</code>) built with <code>_runtime</code> (<code>plan_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_r2c_pass_runtime</code>, <code>plan_c2r_pass_runtime</code>) uses run-time kernels only: RChain, resampled and composite transforms over RChain, the four-step algorithm over RChain. Its type depends on the element type and the number of dimensions, not on the length. The specialised plans are unchanged.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> A library that holds passes of many lengths in one container (as NFFT.jl does for its boxes) has a container of an abstract or length-dependent type, and <code>juliac --trim</code> cannot resolve the calls through it. The first attempt was a keyword <code>runtime = true</code>; the verifier then still checks the specialised branch, whose kernel types depend on the length, so the separate functions were needed. A probe library compiled with <code>juliac --trim=safe</code> holds run-time pass plans in a vector, applies them, and matches the direct DFT to 3·10⁻¹⁴ when called from C. Planning on 64 × 64 × 64 views is 1.8 µs for <code>d = 1</code> and 0.9 µs for <code>d = 2</code> on the M1 (the specialised plan: 4.7 µs and 2.8 µs). Applying the specialised and the run-time plans is within noise of each other on the same views.</p><p><strong>Costs.</strong> For real lengths with large prime factors, the run-time kernel is one resampled transform, without the composite split of the specialised plan.</p><h2 id="The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" tabindex="-1">The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The interface: FFTW.jl&#39;s names, FFTL&#39;s own functions, AbstractFFTs as a backend {#The-interface:-FFTW.jl&#39;s-names,-FFTL&#39;s-own-functions,-AbstractFFTs-as-a-backend}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-06).</strong> FFTL exports FFTW.jl&#39;s names (<code>fft</code>, <code>bfft</code>, <code>ifft</code>, <code>rfft</code>, <code>brfft</code>, <code>irfft</code>, the <code>plan_</code> and in-place forms, the <code>dct</code> family, and the AbstractFFTs helpers), so <code>using FFTL</code> can take the place of <code>using FFTW</code>. <code>r2r</code>, the kinds, the flags and <code>set_num_threads</code> stay public with the prefix, as in FFTW.jl. The DFT and DCT functions are FFTL&#39;s own, not AbstractFFTs&#39;s; the package only does <code>import AbstractFFTs</code>, never <code>using AbstractFFTs</code> or <code>import AbstractFFTs: fft</code>. Generic code that calls <code>AbstractFFTs.fft</code> reaches FFTL through methods of <code>plan_fft</code> and its relatives for <code>AbstractArray</code>s of floating-point numbers (<code>src/abstractffts.jl</code>), which are less specific than FFTW.jl&#39;s <code>StridedArray</code> methods: with FFTW loaded, the generic functions take FFTW for its arrays and FFTL for the rest.</p><p><strong>Why, and what was rejected.</strong></p><ul><li><p>Exported names that are AbstractFFTs&#39;s functions (re-exported bindings) made <code>FFTL.fft === FFTW.fft</code>: any dependency that loads FFTW would take the standard arrays away from code that wrote <code>using FFTL</code>, and the GPU extension&#39;s methods would have landed next to CUDA.jl&#39;s.</p></li><li><p>Methods with FFTW.jl&#39;s exact signatures: the package loaded last overwrites the other for the whole process, and a package depending on both fails to precompile (&quot;Method overwriting is not permitted during Module precompilation&quot;).</p></li><li><p>Methods are global per process, so this is the only arrangement in which a scope with <code>using FFTL</code> reaches FFTL whatever a dependency loads. Tested: with <code>B -&gt; FFTL</code> and <code>B -&gt; A -&gt; FFTW</code>, B gets FFTL and A gets FFTW; a scope that loads both must qualify the name.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#Scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope {#Scope}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Decision (2026-11-01).</strong> Real transforms (r2c, c2r, all real-to-real kinds including halfcomplex), a KernelAbstractions GPU path (complex; Metal and CUDA tuned), a trimmed C library (<code>libfftl</code>, all CPU transforms) and, from 2026-11-02, generic float types are part of FFTL.</p><p><strong>Why.</strong> Real passes are needed by NFFT.jl, and the GPU path reuses the generator and the plan structure. The parts that do not depend on the backend (codelet generation, factorisation, twiddles, pass structure) stay separate from the CPU execution so that the GPU path can share them.</p>',66)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,m as default};
